  "abstract": {
    "sections": [
      {
        "label": "Background",
        "text": "Abstract background sentence. A short-term, in vivo transcriptomic study was used to assess the biological potency of Test Article."
      },
      {
        "label": "Methods",
        "text": "A short-term in vivo biological potency study on Test Article in adult male and female Sprague Dawley rats was conducted. Test Article was formulated in corn oil and administered once daily for 5 consecutive days by gavage (study days 0–4). Test Article was administered at 4 doses (0, 3, 10, and 30 mg/kg body weight [mg/kg]). On study day 5, the day after the final dose was administered, animals were euthanized, standard toxicological measures were assessed. Modeling was conducted to identify the benchmark doses (BMDs) associated with apical toxicological endpoints. A benchmark response of one standard deviation was used to model all endpoints."
      },
      {
        "label": "Results",
        "text": "In male rats, there were no apical endpoints for which a BMD value could be reliably estimated. In female rats, there were no apical endpoints for which a BMD value could be reliably estimated."
      },
      {
        "label": "Summary",
        "text": ""
      }
    ]
  },
